# Code Quality

## Fix properly, never patch
- Diagnose the root cause before editing. Fix the actual source, even if it means touching more files or refactoring shared code.
- No band-aids: no swallowed exceptions, no magic special-cases, no "TODO fix later", no silencing errors to make a test pass.
- If a proper fix needs the extra mile (schema change, interface change, migration), do it. Explain the trade-off in one line.

## Reuse before you write
- Search for existing helpers/utilities/types before adding new ones. Extend or import instead of duplicating.
- Extract repeated logic (used 2+ times) into a shared function/module.
- Match existing patterns, naming, and error-handling conventions in the codebase.

## Finish the job
- Complete the full task before yielding. No half-done functions, stubbed returns, or unfinished branches left behind.
- After substantive edits, run linters/tests and fix what you introduced.
